these are just a few of the tech  companies whose share prices have fallen  in 2022  and here's a chart of the percentage  change in the tech heavy nasdaq compared  to broader indexes while stocks are down  in general the tech industry is having  an especially hard time and in april and  may a wave of layoffs and hiring freezes  hit the sector the story of what's  happening with tech is not just a story  about this year or even the last year  itself it's really a story about what's  been happening over the past decade  while other sectors have faced declines  here's why the tech industry is having a  record downturn after years of growth  ten years ago the tech industry was  ready to grow the u.s was still  recovering from the great recession and  interest rates were hovering near zero  the federal reserve is using its  monetary policy tools to promote a more  robust recovery so low interest rates  they're good for tech stocks uh lower  yields make it really really easy to  you know  increase the appeal of the future  earnings that a lot of these tech stocks  will have in 2012 and 2013 tech players  like facebook and twitter went public  and advancements like mobile and cloud  technology helped drive profits for many  companies and fueled growth in the  sector  let's go back to that nasdaq chart we  can see how the tech industry steadily  grew over the decade as money poured in  outpacing growth in other industries  as revenue grew companies also expanded  their workforces for example facebook  added about 40 000 employees to the  company between 2012 and 2019. by the  end of that year tech was at the  forefront of the stock market with a  bull run being driven by companies like  apple and microsoft 2010s were really  the decade of disruption for a lot of  these tech stocks we really started to  you know be on our phones longer we  started going on social media and all  these factors combined really really  increased the allure of tech stocks and  investors wanted a piece of it low  interest rates and increased smartphone  use were vital contributors to growth  those two factors combined together  really made it easy to you know just  sort of go into the tech trade and  really led to the valuations of a lot of  these tech companies to become really  inflated really lofty then 2020 hit the  breaking news stay at home that is the  order tonight from four state governors  stocks across the board initially took a  plunge as stay-at-home orders and  lockdowns rippled across the world  with more people stuck at home the tech  industry was set up to capitalize on  demand when the pandemic first started  we had all these lockdowns we had travel  restrictions and people were stuck at  home so what do you do when you're stuck  at home you go online and you binge  watch shows on netflix you want to talk  to your friends you go to zoom at the  same time washington enacted a massive  stimulus plan the government sent out  more than 150 million stimulus checks  and the fed made plans to lend billions  of dollars across markets we have held  our policy rate near zero since  mid-march and have stated that we will  keep it there until we are confident  that the economy has weathered recent  events and is on track to achieve our  maximum employment and price stability  goals the federal reserve slashed  interest rates and they were  historically ultra low and that made it  also very very easy for a lot of these  tech companies to take on cash to take  on you know take to borrow more money  and lower their borrowing costs so yeah  that all together really helped tech  stocks get to the levels they were at in  2020 and 2021. as stimulus flooded the  economy market cap prices for tech  leaders like amazon apple and meta  reached new records some companies  significantly grew their workforces to  meet rising demand announcing increased  hiring during earnings calls to  shareholders we ended the quarter with  over 71 900 full-time employees up 23  compared to last year we finished the  quarter with nearly 3 800 employees up  134 year-over-year but growing  valuations flash a warning sign to some  analysts who speculated that companies  might not be able to keep up with the  exploding growth  inflation was beginning to take center  stage we had federal reserve chair  jerome powell come in and say that yeah  okay we're now going to start thinking  about raising rates to fight inflation  because it seems like it isn't temporary  anymore high inflation is is a is a  severe threat to the achievement of  maximum employment into achieving a long  expansion that can give us that and  that's an investor's absolutely freaking  out panicking about these tech companies  share prices for small tech companies  and giants alike started to slip  while inflation supply chain issues and  the war in ukraine were impacting  businesses around the world tech was  being faced with other unique challenges  ad revenue which social media companies  like snap twitter and facebook rely on  has taken a hit as inflation led  companies to spend less on advertising  and apple introduced a privacy feature  last year that restricts user tracking  which means that sites have a harder  time targeting ads to users companies  now had to redesign their ad targeting  and tracking tools to comply with the  new rules and by doing so lost business  meta estimated that apple's ad tracking  changes would cost the company 10  billion dollars this year another issue  that caused earnings to take a tumble is  changing consumer behaviors so  previously we were all at home ordering  stuff online again and now we're  shifting back and going back in person  we're going to restaurants we're going  to stores companies are looking to cut  costs having expanded when growth was  booming in a shareholder meeting meta  said that given the resulting revenue  head headwinds we have adjusted our  plans for hiring an expense growth this  year carvana ceo said in an email that  they would cut around 2 500 workers and  robin hood said in a shareholder meeting  on their increased hiring that at the  time we thought that this was right to  keep up with the pace of the business  but it also led to some inefficiency  some redundant roles  stocks have dropped quickly amid poor  earnings results and the slowdown in  growth  now some investors are bailing on  volatile tech stocks as interest rates  rise so tech companies also include some  of the most speculative companies in the  market and riskier ones as well so it  makes it really really hard for you to  justify owning something that's not  going to be profitable for a long time  in the future to come it's unclear where  the market might go from here analysts  and investors right now remain divided  about whether this slowdown is going to  be temporary or permanent and not all  tech companies have been hit as hard ibm  and citrix have both continued to  outperform the declining market but with  the federal reserve expected to steeply  raise interest rates analysts say tech  stocks will continue to suffer

